From: pocket-debs
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 07:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] mtkisp50: pipeline handler and IPA for the MediaTek MT6785 ISP
Description:
 A pipeline handler of its own for the MediaTek MT6785 camera subsystem
 with the ISP pass 2 (DIP) as memory-to-memory ISP, and its IPA. It
 replaces the DIP backend of the software ISP (patch 0004) step by step;
 until then both are built and the new handler only matches when it is
 selected (pipelines_match_list, or pipelines.mtkisp50.enabled in the
 configuration file). Without selection nothing changes.
 .
 - Statistics buffer layout (mtkisp50/stats.h) shaped like a hardware
   statistics unit: cell grid up to 128x128 with means, sample and
   saturation counts, histograms, focus blocks with bands, raw frame
   sequence and timestamp, phase detection results of a grid of windows.
 - IPA interface (mtkisp50.mojom) and IPA module: the algorithms of the
   simple IPA with the MediaTek extensions, unchanged apart from their
   types, the DIP parameter encoder of 0004, a statistics view that
   parses the statistics buffer; DIP tuning from the algorithms ToneCurve,
   LensShadingCorrection and NoiseReduction; AeState; exposure, white
   balance and lens position kept over a new configuration of the same
   camera. Uncalibrated tuning without image quality blocks. Image quality
   sets per job (preview, video, still image).
 - Exposure programs per use (tuning block "program" of Agc): the total
   exposure is split into stages of exposure time and largest gain, times
   in steps free of mains flicker, analogue before digital gain, within
   the frame durations of the application; a target over the brightness
   of the scene and a limit for highlights.
 - Exposure time for images taken by hand (key "handheld" of the program
   block, [time, total gain]): the preview and still image programs hold
   the exposure time at that time up to that total gain, darker scenes
   take the stages beyond; the video program keeps its own. Without the
   key as before.
 - White balance in dim light: channel means averaged per unit of
   exposure over a few frames and judged against their noise, the last
   estimate held below it, the first one taken at once; module
   sensitivity; a preference that keeps part of the colour of warm light
   (applied after the colour matrix); colour matrix strength over the
   gain.
 - White balance under a light whose colour drifts: with colourError
   "whitepoint" the white point is fitted exactly to the curve (steps of
   0.25 mired within the range of the AwbMode, refined by a parabola,
   offset across the curve within transversePos/transverseNeg); the
   tuning block "rendering" (band, speed, switch in mired) keeps the
   colour temperature of the colour matrix and the preference within a
   band of the estimate, lets it follow slowly beyond and all the way for
   a new light beyond "switch", while the gains follow the estimate (the
   metadata report the colour temperature of the rendering); bright
   zones above "maxZoneLevel" (lamps) are left out of the means. Without
   these keys as before. The statistics zones go to a debug category of
   their own and can be replayed through the algorithms.
 - Temporal noise reduction of the preview and video jobs (tuning block
   TemporalNoiseReduction, settings per sensitivity, off without it):
   still image jobs without it; a new reference after jumps of exposure,
   white balance and scene brightness and while the lens moves; only for
   a driver whose parameter buffers have the size of the header, and with
   at least three buffers of a main stream.
 - Multi-frame still images (tuning block MultiFrame and the image
   quality sets multiFrameInput/multiFrameBase/multiFrameOutput, off
   without them, and only with the multi-frame blending block (MFB) and
   the blend inputs of the DIP): where the sensitivity calls for it, the
   still image is the first frame of a series the handler captures on its
   own with exposure, colour and lens held; the frames go through the DIP,
   an alignment module (sharpest frame as base, global and local motion,
   unreliable blocks marked) aligns them, the MFB blends them one after the
   other with a weight map, then two DIP jobs with NV12 input: the noise
   reduction of the base frame and the blend of both over the weight map
   into the buffer of the still image. The DIP and the MFB do not run at
   the same time. Fallbacks: below the threshold, without the blocks, with
   too little camera memory or with draft::NoiseReductionMode fast a
   single frame; failed frames and unusable alignments fall out, without a
   blend the base frame alone; a capture that times out goes on with the
   frames back; a stop during the series returns everything.
 - Calibration of the single camera module: the tuning holds the values
   of a reference module, an optional file <sensor>-unit.yaml in
   ipa/mtkisp50 below localstatedir/lib/libcamera (or the directory of
   LIBCAMERA_MTKISP50_UNIT_DIR) the values of the module (version 1, a
   list of algorithms); its keys replace the top level keys of the
   algorithms of the same name, algorithms the tuning does not have are
   left out. Without the file, with an invalid one or with values the
   algorithms refuse the tuning alone; the log names the keys taken.
 - Pipeline handler: capture by sensor, seninf and CAMSV into an internal
   raw pool or the raw stream, DIP job by DMABUF (raw, parameters, NV12
   main output, YUYV scaled output), CPU statistics (copy of the software
   ISP statistics writing the statistics buffer) in a thread of their own.
   Up to two processed streams and one raw stream (MediaTek packed 10-bit)
   per camera; raw only for sensors the DIP does not take; one camera at
   a time. For sensors that send phase detection (PD) pixels on a stream
   of their own (OV64B40 in the large mode) a second route through seninf
   to a free CAMSV, PD frames paired with raw frames by timestamp and PD
   statistics on the CPU; other modes and kernels without the PD stream as
   before, the routing goes back when the camera is released.
 - Phase detection autofocus (tuning block "pd" of Af): a search moves the
   lens to the PD target and refines it with a short contrast search;
   while focused the lens only moves when PD results in a row depart from
   the PD target seen in focus by more than a deadband; a change of the
   light alone starts no search while PD sees the subject in focus;
   without reliable PD the contrast search and watch as before.
 - Streams: NV12 on the main output (a crop of the sensor mode, as
   before), YUYV on the scaled output (1/2 to 1/16 of the processed area,
   for instance 1920x1080 video from 16:9 of the whole large mode). With
   viewfinder and still capture in one configuration the still is a buffer
   in a request, without a new configuration of the camera. A job with
   other outputs than the streaming ones waits until the ISP is idle, then
   the outputs are switched.
 - Controls: AeEnable, ExposureTimeMode/AnalogueGainMode with
   ExposureTime/AnalogueGain, ExposureValue, AeMeteringMode,
   FrameDurationLimits (also as a control of the start), AwbEnable,
   AwbMode, ColourGains, ColourTemperature, AfMode/AfTrigger/AfWindows/
   AfPause, LensPosition, ScalerCrop and ScalerCropMaximum,
   draft::NoiseReductionMode (with multi-frame tuning);
   AeState also for small steps at the limits;
   FrameDuration in the metadata.
 - Frame length: the exposure lengthens the frame (VBLANK) up to a tuned
   factor, within the frame durations of the application; the reported
   limits are the ones the regulation reaches in the mode. A scene too
   dark for the focus measure does not hold the frame length while the
   continuous autofocus waits.
 - Mode changes (preview to photo and back) keep exposure (with the frame
   length), white balance, lens position and focus state; no new search
   after the return. Optional zone metering after the lens shading.
 - Requests wait for a raw buffer of the pool; smaller pool next to a raw
   stream and for the full sensor mode, so that the capture device keeps a
   spare frame in the camera memory region.
 - Tests: bookkeeping and order of the ISP queues, scheduling of jobs with
   different outputs, stream planning (validate cases), autofocus over a
   new configuration, frame length of the exposure regulation, PD
   statistics (synthetic frames, optional real frames against reference
   results), pairing of PD and raw frames, autofocus with PD, exposure
   programs and the regulation with them, the limit of the exposure time
   by hand, white balance in dim light and
   the preference, the exact fit of the white point, the steady rendering
   under a recorded drifting light, with the calibration of the module
   and for a new light, the temporal noise reduction (restarts, settings
   between the sensitivities, parameter buffers, fallback), the frames of
   a series per sensitivity and the plan of its blends, the calibration
   of the camera module over the tuning (keys replaced, missing, broken
   and unknown files, algorithms the tuning does not have); CPU
   statistics against the software ISP statistics frame by frame.
 .
 Applies on top of 0001, 0003 and 0004.
Forwarded: no
License: LGPL-2.1-or-later (as the files it derives from; the alignment
 module src/libcamera/pipeline/mtkisp50/align/ is LGPL-2.1-or-later as
 well); GPL-2.0-or-later for the tests
Last-Update: 2026-11-22
---
diff --git a/include/libcamera/internal/mtkisp50/stats.h b/include/libcamera/internal/mtkisp50/stats.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..29b7c0d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/include/libcamera/internal/mtkisp50/stats.h
@@ -0,0 +1,176 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-or-later */
+/*
+ * Statistics buffer of the mtkisp50 pipeline
+ *
+ * One buffer per frame, written by the statistics source (the CPU today, a
+ * hardware statistics unit later) and read by the IPA. The layout follows what
+ * a hardware statistics unit of the ISP delivers, so that both sources fill
+ * the same form and the algorithms do not depend on the source:
+ *
+ * - a grid of cells of up to kMaxGridWidth x kMaxGridHeight with the mean of
+ *   each colour channel, the number of samples and the number of saturated
+ *   samples per cell;
+ * - up to kMaxHistograms histograms of up to kMaxHistogramBins bins;
+ * - a grid of focus blocks with a level and up to kMaxAfBands filter bands per
+ *   block;
+ * - the sequence number and timestamp of the raw frame the statistics were
+ *   computed on, as statistics and image may come from different counters;
+ * - the phase detection (PD) result of a grid of windows and of all of them
+ *   together, for sensors that send their phase detection pixels on a
+ *   stream of their own.
+ *
+ * The buffer is plain data without pointers, shared between processes.
+ */
+
+#pragma once
+
+#include <array>
+#include <stdint.h>
+
+namespace libcamera {
+
+namespace mtkisp50 {
+
+enum class StatsSource : uint32_t {
+	None = 0,
+	Cpu = 1,
+	Hardware = 2,
+};
+
+/* Validity flags of the parts of a statistics buffer */
+enum StatsValid : uint32_t {
+	StatsValidCells = 1 << 0,
+	StatsValidHistograms = 1 << 1,
+	StatsValidAf = 1 << 2,
+	StatsValidPd = 1 << 3,
+};
+
+/* Kind of a histogram */
+enum StatsHistogramType : uint32_t {
+	/* Luma of 2x2 samples, 77/150/29 of 256, no pre-gain, 8 bit scale */
+	StatsHistogramLumaBt601 = 1,
+	/* Luma from the configured coefficients and pre-gains */
+	StatsHistogramLuma = 2,
+};
+
+/* Meaning of the focus block values */
+enum StatsAfMeasure : uint32_t {
+	/* level = sum of the green samples of the block, no filter bands */
+	StatsAfLevelSum = 1,
+	/* band[] = filter responses of the block, level = mean green */
+	StatsAfFilterBands = 2,
+};
+
+struct StatsCell {
+	/* Mean of the R, G and B samples of the cell, in raw sample units */
+	double mean[3];
+	/* Number of samples (2x2 Bayer cells) */
+	uint32_t count;
+	/* Number of samples with a saturated channel, 0 if not measured */
+	uint32_t saturated;
+};
+
+struct StatsHistogram {
+	uint32_t type;
+	uint32_t bins;
+	uint32_t data[256];
+};
+
+struct StatsAfBlock {
+	uint32_t level;
+	uint32_t band[2];
+};
+
+/*
+ * Phase detection result of a window. The shift is the distance in pixels of
+ * the sensor output frame between the images of the left and the right phase
+ * detection pixels: zero in focus, the sign tells the direction of the lens
+ * move. The confidence (0..100) grows with the depth of the match minimum and
+ * the contrast, and drops with saturation.
+ */
+struct StatsPdWindow {
+	/* Window in pixels of the sensor output frame */
+	uint32_t x, y, width, height;
+	float shift;
+	float confidence;
+	/* Mean absolute difference of neighbouring profile samples, relative */
+	float contrast;
+	/* Fraction of saturated phase detection samples */
+	float saturation;
+	/* Mean level of the samples above the black level, raw sample units */
+	float level;
+	/* Curvature of the match at its minimum */
+	float curvature;
+};
+
+struct StatsBuffer {
+	static constexpr uint32_t kVersion = 2;
+
+	static constexpr unsigned int kMaxGridWidth = 128;
+	static constexpr unsigned int kMaxGridHeight = 128;
+	static constexpr unsigned int kMaxHistograms = 4;
+	static constexpr unsigned int kMaxHistogramBins = 256;
+	/* The CPU focus grid of the main camera reaches 160 cells per line */
+	static constexpr unsigned int kMaxAfWidth = 160;
+	static constexpr unsigned int kMaxAfHeight = 128;
+	static constexpr unsigned int kMaxAfBands = 2;
+	static constexpr unsigned int kMaxPdWindows = 25;
+
+	uint32_t version;
+	uint32_t source;
+	/* Frame (request sequence) the statistics belong to */
+	uint32_t frame;
+	/* Sequence number and timestamp of the raw frame */
+	uint32_t rawSequence;
+	uint64_t rawTimestamp;
+
+	/* StatsValid flags; 0 if nothing was measured for this frame */
+	uint32_t valid;
+	/* Statistics left out on purpose for this frame (longer interval) */
+	uint32_t skipped;
+
+	/*
+	 * Bit depth of the cell means. Focus levels of the CPU statistics
+	 * are sums of 8 bit samples.
+	 */
+	uint32_t bitDepth;
+	/* Black level already subtracted from the means (0 or 1), and its value */
+	uint32_t blackLevelSubtracted;
+	uint32_t blackLevel;
+	/* Luma coefficients (of 16) and pre-gains (1.0 = 512) applied */
+	uint16_t yCoeffs[3];
+	uint16_t preGains[3];
+
+	/* Cell grid: window in raw frame pixels and number of cells */
+	uint32_t windowX, windowY, windowWidth, windowHeight;
+	uint32_t gridWidth, gridHeight;
+
+	uint32_t histogramCount;
+
+	/* Focus blocks: window, block size in pixels, samples per block */
+	uint32_t afX, afY;
+	uint32_t afWidth, afHeight;
+	uint32_t afBlockSize;
+	uint32_t afBlockSamples;
+	uint32_t afMeasure;
+	uint32_t afBands;
+
+	std::array<StatsCell, kMaxGridWidth * kMaxGridHeight> cells;
+	std::array<StatsHistogram, kMaxHistograms> histograms;
+	std::array<StatsAfBlock, kMaxAfWidth * kMaxAfHeight> af;
+
+	/*
+	 * Phase detection (StatsValidPd): sequence and timestamp of the
+	 * phase detection frame, grid of windows (rows from the top) and the
+	 * result of all windows together
+	 */
+	uint32_t pdSequence;
+	uint64_t pdTimestamp;
+	uint32_t pdGridWidth, pdGridHeight;
+	StatsPdWindow pdRegion;
+	std::array<StatsPdWindow, kMaxPdWindows> pd;
+};
+
+} /* namespace mtkisp50 */
+
+} /* namespace libcamera */
